{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments ["<b>As an eagle stirs up his nest.</b> He guided them with mercy and compassion, like this eagle which is compassionate toward its young, and does not enter its nest suddenly, but rather flutters and beats over its young with its wings, from tree to tree, from thicket to thicket, so that its young wake up and have strength to receive him (Sifrei):" "<b>Stirs up his nest.</b> He rouses his young:" "<b>Hovering over its fledglings.</b> He does not press his weight upon them but hovers — touching and not touching (Jerusalem Talmud, Ḥagigah 2) — so too the Holy One, blessed be He: ״שַׁדַּי לֹא מְצָאנֻהוּ שַׂגִּיא כֹחַ״ (Job 37) — when He came to give the Torah He did not reveal Himself to them from one direction alone but from four directions, as it says: ״ה' מִסִּינַי בָּא וְזָרַח מִשֵּׂעִיר לָמוֹ הוֹפִיעַ מֵהַר פָּארָן״ (Deuteronomy 33), ״אֱלוֹהַּ מִתֵּימָן יָבוֹא״ (Habakkuk 3) — this is the fourth direction (Sifrei):" "<b>He spreads his wings, he takes him.</b> When he comes to take them from place to place he does not carry them in his feet like other birds, because other birds fear the eagle since it flies high and soars above them; therefore they carry their young in their feet, out of fear of the eagle. But the eagle fears only the arrow; therefore it carries them on its wings — it says: let the arrow rather enter me and not enter my young. So too the Holy One, blessed be He (Exodus 19): ״וָאֶשָּׂא אֶתְכֶם עַל כַּנְפֵי נְשָׁרִים״ — when Egypt journeyed after them and caught up with them at the sea, they were shooting arrows and catapult-stones at them. Immediately: ״וַיִּסַּע מַלְאַךְ הָאֱלֹהִים״ etc. ״וַיָּבֹא בֵּין מַחֲנֵה מִצְרַיִם״ etc. (Exodus 14):"], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T08:49:16.341207652Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}